1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,500
A consumer group tracked 201 Black
Friday deals for an entire year.

2
00:00:05,540 --> 00:00:07,740
They checked the price every single day,

3
00:00:07,780 --> 00:00:10,380
six months before and six months after.

4
00:00:10,420 --> 00:00:15,820
Out of 201 deals, only one was
actually cheapest on Black Friday.

5
00:00:15,860 --> 00:00:19,100
One. And this year, the stakes are higher.

6
00:00:19,140 --> 00:00:24,620
Prices are up 3.7% from last year,
gas is sitting around $4.20 a gallon,

7
00:00:24,660 --> 00:00:29,740
and 44% of Americans already expect
to start next year in holiday debt.

8
00:00:29,780 --> 00:00:31,580
Stores know you're stretched.

9
00:00:31,620 --> 00:00:36,060
That's exactly why Black Friday
2026 is built to trap you.

10
00:00:36,100 --> 00:00:38,620
Here are the five traps,
the numbers behind them,

11
00:00:38,660 --> 00:00:40,700
and how to walk out with your money.

12
00:00:40,740 --> 00:00:42,446
This is WorthWired.

13
00:00:42,696 --> 00:00:46,096
Trap number one. The fake "was" price.

14
00:00:46,136 --> 00:00:48,656
You see a TV. Was $999?

15
00:00:48,696 --> 00:00:52,656
Now $599. Your brain locks
onto that first number.

16
00:00:52,696 --> 00:00:54,096
It's called anchoring.

17
00:00:54,136 --> 00:00:56,896
And suddenly, the lower
price feels like winning.

18
00:00:56,936 --> 00:00:59,536
But was it ever really $999?

19
00:00:59,576 --> 00:01:01,616
The UK consumer group Which?

20
00:01:01,656 --> 00:01:06,816
found that 99.5% of Black Friday deals
were the same price or cheaper at some

21
00:01:06,856 --> 00:01:08,096
other time of the year.

22
00:01:08,136 --> 00:01:12,416
92% were already that price or
lower in the six months before.

23
00:01:12,456 --> 00:01:17,616
In the US, the FTC's own pricing guides
say a former price is deceptive if it's

24
00:01:17,656 --> 00:01:21,056
fictitious, set high just
to create a big markdown.

25
00:01:21,096 --> 00:01:24,096
The rule exists, the tricks still happen.

26
00:01:24,136 --> 00:01:27,376
The fix. Before you buy anything over $50,

27
00:01:27,416 --> 00:01:28,736
check its price history.

28
00:01:28,776 --> 00:01:31,856
Price trackers show you every
price it's had for months.

29
00:01:31,896 --> 00:01:34,568
If the deal is the
normal price, walk away.

30
00:01:34,978 --> 00:01:37,018
Trap number two: the countdown.

31
00:01:37,058 --> 00:01:40,458
Only three left. Deal ends in 14 minutes.

32
00:01:40,498 --> 00:01:43,818
Doorbusters, flashing
timers, limited quantities.

33
00:01:43,858 --> 00:01:46,218
None of that changes what
the product is worth.

34
00:01:46,258 --> 00:01:48,378
It changes how fast you decide.

35
00:01:48,418 --> 00:01:51,578
Urgency is designed to skip the
part of your brain that asks,

36
00:01:51,618 --> 00:01:53,178
Do I actually need this?

37
00:01:53,218 --> 00:01:54,858
And remember what the data said:

38
00:01:54,898 --> 00:01:57,178
the same price usually comes back.

39
00:01:57,218 --> 00:01:59,418
The fix is the 24-hour rule:

40
00:01:59,458 --> 00:02:02,378
put it in your cart, close
the app, and sleep on it.

41
00:02:02,418 --> 00:02:04,698
If you still want it tomorrow
and it's on your list,

42
00:02:04,738 --> 00:02:07,078
buy it. Most of the time, you won't.

43
00:02:07,349 --> 00:02:10,029
Trap number three, pay in four.

44
00:02:10,069 --> 00:02:12,749
Four easy payments, zero interest.

45
00:02:12,789 --> 00:02:17,629
It sounds harmless, and 39% of Americans
say they'll use it or consider it this

46
00:02:17,669 --> 00:02:22,189
holiday season. But the CFPB, the
Federal Consumer Protection Agency,

47
00:02:22,229 --> 00:02:23,469
studied these loans.

48
00:02:23,509 --> 00:02:27,709
More than 60% of borrowers had several
of them going at the same time.

49
00:02:27,749 --> 00:02:30,109
A third used more than one company.

50
00:02:30,149 --> 00:02:32,589
So no single app sees the whole picture,

51
00:02:32,629 --> 00:02:33,789
and neither do you.

52
00:02:33,829 --> 00:02:37,469
$40 here, $60 there, $25 on another app,

53
00:02:37,509 --> 00:02:41,149
and by January, half your
paycheck is already spoken for.

54
00:02:41,189 --> 00:02:45,789
Lenders approved 78% of these loans
for people with subprime credit.

55
00:02:45,829 --> 00:02:48,349
The fix. If you can't pay for it today,

56
00:02:48,389 --> 00:02:50,589
you can't afford it in four parts.

57
00:02:50,629 --> 00:02:54,101
And never have more than one
pay-later plan open at a time.

58
00:02:54,351 --> 00:02:57,591
Trap number four and
the most expensive one.

59
00:02:57,631 --> 00:03:00,871
Save 20% today when you open a store card.

60
00:03:00,911 --> 00:03:04,311
Let's do the math. A $750 TV.

61
00:03:04,351 --> 00:03:06,871
You open the card, you save 20%.

62
00:03:06,911 --> 00:03:09,991
That's $150 off. You pay $600.

63
00:03:10,031 --> 00:03:15,431
Feels great. But the average store credit
card charges over 30% interest according

64
00:03:15,471 --> 00:03:21,031
to Bankrate. If you pay $30 a month, it
takes you 29 months to pay off that TV

65
00:03:21,071 --> 00:03:24,131
and you pay $244 in interest.

66
00:03:24,351 --> 00:03:28,151
You saved $150. You paid $244.

67
00:03:28,191 --> 00:03:30,311
The store didn't give you a discount.

68
00:03:30,351 --> 00:03:32,311
It sold you a loan.

69
00:03:32,351 --> 00:03:37,031
The fix. Only open a store card if you'll
pay the full balance on the very first

70
00:03:37,071 --> 00:03:40,372
statement. Otherwise,
the discount is the bait.

71
00:03:40,622 --> 00:03:43,862
Trap number five. Spend more to save more.

72
00:03:43,902 --> 00:03:46,342
Free shipping over $75.

73
00:03:46,382 --> 00:03:52,102
Buy two, get one. Spend $100, get a $20
gift card you can only use in January

74
00:03:52,142 --> 00:03:55,862
at the same store. Each one
pushes your total up a little.

75
00:03:55,902 --> 00:04:02,182
And it adds up. A 2026 survey by
Omnisend found that 44% of Americans

76
00:04:02,222 --> 00:04:04,902
expect to carry holiday
debt into next year,

77
00:04:04,942 --> 00:04:07,702
averaging $408 per person.

78
00:04:07,742 --> 00:04:10,982
That's almost $49 billion nationwide.

79
00:04:11,022 --> 00:04:14,662
With holiday groceries
costing more and gas over $4,

80
00:04:14,702 --> 00:04:16,502
that debt doesn't stay small.

81
00:04:16,542 --> 00:04:20,243
It follows you into a year where
everything already costs more.

82
00:04:20,733 --> 00:04:23,173
So here's your Black Friday playbook.

83
00:04:23,213 --> 00:04:25,413
One. Make the list now.

84
00:04:25,453 --> 00:04:28,293
Names, gifts, and a dollar limit for each.

85
00:04:28,333 --> 00:04:30,773
If it's not on the list, it's not a deal.

86
00:04:30,813 --> 00:04:35,493
Two. Check the price history
before you buy anything over $50.

87
00:04:35,533 --> 00:04:38,133
Three. Use the 24-hour rule.

88
00:04:38,173 --> 00:04:39,973
Cart it, sleep on it.

89
00:04:40,013 --> 00:04:44,133
Four. Pay with cash, debit, or
a credit card you pay in full.

90
00:04:44,173 --> 00:04:47,253
No store cards, no
stacked pay-later plans.

91
00:04:47,293 --> 00:04:51,413
Five. Set one total holiday
budget and track it like a bill.

92
00:04:51,453 --> 00:04:53,233
When it's gone, you're done.

93
00:04:53,494 --> 00:04:55,534
Black Friday isn't evil.

94
00:04:55,574 --> 00:04:59,934
Real deals exist. But the biggest discount
you'll get this year is the one you

95
00:04:59,974 --> 00:05:02,894
give yourself by not paying for the trap.

96
00:05:02,934 --> 00:05:05,854
Comment the worst deal a
store ever talked you into.

97
00:05:05,894 --> 00:05:09,614
And if this saved you even 50 bucks,
send it to someone who's about to open a

98
00:05:09,654 --> 00:05:12,407
store card. This is WorthWired.

